          <div class="prose-blog">
            <p>Bangkok has music rehearsal rooms spread across multiple districts — some near the BTS and MRT, others tucked into quieter side streets. Most provide full equipment so you don't need to haul gear. Quality and pricing vary considerably. This guide covers where to find rehearsal rooms in Bangkok, what equipment to expect, how to choose the right one, and what to know before your first visit.</p>

            <h2>What is a rehearsal room?</h2>
            <p>A rehearsal room is a soundproofed, hourly-rental space built for bands and musicians. Thick walls mean you won't disturb neighbors, and equipment is included so you don't need to invest in your own. It's the practical middle ground between practicing at home with headphones and booking a full recording studio. For a deeper explanation of how rehearsal rooms work, see <a href={`/${lang}/blog/what-is-a-rehearsal-room`}>What is a rehearsal room?</a></p>

            <h2>Where to find rehearsal rooms in Bangkok</h2>
            <p>Rehearsal studios are distributed unevenly across the city. Here's what to expect by area:</p>

            <p><strong>Ladprao / MRT corridor</strong> — Several studios are spread along this corridor and accessible by MRT. A practical option for musicians based in central Bangkok or the eastern suburbs.</p>

            <p><strong>Sukhumvit / BTS</strong> — Studios in this area exist but tend to be smaller, with higher rates due to premium real estate. Convenient location if members are spread across the city center.</p>

            <p><strong>Bang Khen (north Bangkok)</strong> — Home to <strong>Thunder Studio</strong>. Quieter neighborhood, easy parking, and straightforward road access. Well suited for bands arriving by car. See the <a href={`/${lang}/location`}>location page</a> for directions.</p>

            <p><strong>Siam / Ratchathewi</strong> — Centrally located and easy to reach from any direction, but rehearsal spaces here are limited. Rooms tend to be compact.</p>

            <h2>Equipment in Bangkok rehearsal rooms</h2>
            <p>A well-equipped rehearsal room should include:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Marshall amps</strong> — guitar and bass amplification. Standard in most Bangkok rehearsal rooms.</li>
              <li><strong>Full drum kit</strong> — bass drum, snare, hi-hat, cymbals, and toms as a complete setup.</li>
              <li><strong>PA system</strong> — for vocals and overall mix monitoring.</li>
              <li><strong>Microphones</strong> — with stands and cables.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Thunder Studio offers a <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/large-room`}>large room (Studio 1)</a> with Marshall amps, full drum kit, and PA system, a <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/small-room`}>small room (Studio 2)</a> for smaller groups or solo practice, and a <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/control-room`}>Control Room</a> for recording and mixing sessions.</p>

            <h2>How to choose a rehearsal room in Bangkok</h2>
            <p>The most important factor is group size:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>1-4 people</strong> — a small room is sufficient. No reason to pay for a large room.</li>
              <li><strong>5-10 people</strong> — you need a large room with space to move and a full equipment setup.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Other things to evaluate: whether the room includes a drum kit, parking availability, opening hours, and how far each band member needs to travel. For a detailed comparison, read the <a href={`/${lang}/blog/choosing-room-size`}>rehearsal room size guide</a>.</p>

